-- Newsroom drafts for 2026-11-20 (Friday). Loaded once into EDITORIAL_REVIEW; an editor approves before anything is published.
-- Facts restated in Boston Daily's own words with attribution. Every story carries its own photo; none was used in an earlier package or open draft (grepped content/, seed/ and the open newsroom/2026-11-19 and content/thanksgiving-2026 branches).
-- loc.gov and Wikimedia Commons could not be opened from the newsroom sandbox on this run. Photo URLs are Wikimedia Commons thumbnails built from file names confirmed by search (MD5 path). One is a U.S. House official portrait (public domain); eight carry a Commons license that the editor should confirm on the file page. The image check falls back to 800px or the original file if a thumbnail size is missing.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261120-senate-passes-baaja', 'senate-passes-baaja-permitting-grid-bill-62-33-sends-to-house', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'congress', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Senate passes the bipartisan permitting and power-grid overhaul 62-33, sending it to the House', 'The vote on Thursday, Nov. 19, followed a blocked effort to add amendments. Speaker Mike Johnson predicts he has the votes, but House members in both parties want changes with only weeks left in the term.', 'The Senate on Thursday, Nov. 19, passed the Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act, a broad rewrite of federal permitting rules for energy, mining and infrastructure projects, by a vote of 62-33, The Hill and Bloomberg reported. The bill now goes to the House.

## What the bill does

Bloomberg described the measure as a roughly 500-page compromise negotiated with the White House, which supports it. According to The Hill, it is meant to speed construction of power lines and other projects, including by letting federal regulators approve transmission lines when states do not act. It would bar presidents from pulling permits that have already been issued, exempt some activities such as natural gas pipeline expansions from environmental reviews, and limit lawsuits to 150 days after approval and to people who took part in the formal comment period. It would also make data centers pay for the grid costs they create.

Sen. Katie Britt (R-Ala.), who voted yes, said in a statement that the bill sets firm deadlines for reviews while keeping environmental and preservation standards, and urged the House to send it to the president without delay.

## How senators split

Only two Republicans, Sens. Mike Rounds of South Dakota and Josh Hawley of Missouri, voted no, The Hill reported. Democrats were divided. Those voting yes included Minority Leader Chuck Schumer and Sens. Sheldon Whitehouse, Martin Heinrich, Chris Coons, John Fetterman, Amy Klobuchar and Angus King, an independent.

No amendments were adopted. Sen. Shelley Moore Capito (R-W.Va.) asked for consent to take up a bipartisan package of amendments, but Sen. Jeff Merkley (D-Ore.) objected, and he and Sen. Ron Wyden (D-Ore.) blocked the amendment process, according to The Hill. Wyden had sought to bar data center construction on public lands. Oil and gas and clean-energy groups back the bill, while many major environmental groups and some tribes oppose it, Bloomberg reported.

## The House outlook

Bloomberg reported that Speaker Mike Johnson predicted he has the votes, while Democratic Leader Hakeem Jeffries and members of both parties want changes. Rep. Bruce Westerman (R-Ark.), who chairs the House Natural Resources Committee, doubted there was time to rewrite the bill before the term ends in December and said the House would likely have to pass the Senate version. The Hill noted a crowded year-end calendar that includes a possible lapse in government funding.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/High_voltage_transmission_towers_and_lines.jpg/1280px-High_voltage_transmission_towers_and_lines.jpg', 'High-voltage transmission towers and lines (file). Photo via Wikimedia Commons (editor to confirm author and license on the file page)', 'licensed', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-20.sql', 9, 1, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-20T11:00:00.000Z', '2026-11-20T11:00:00.000Z');
